\section{Introduction}
This document presents a simulation-focused study of the Apex-TI v4.0
Virtual Quantum Substrate (VQS), emphasizing numerical behavior, topological
phase transitions, and distributed memory stability under stress.

\section{Simulation Model}
The virtual Hamiltonian is defined as
\[
H_{ij} = t_{ij} + \rho_d f(i,j) + \eta g(i,j),
\]
with:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $t_{ij}$: hopping amplitudes,
    \item $\rho_d$: defect density,
    \item $\eta$: spectral perturbation scale.
\end{itemize}

The condition number
\[
\kappa = \frac{\sigma_{\max}}{\sigma_{\min}}
\]
is monitored at each simulation step.

\section{Numerical Setup}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Lattice size: $N = 50\,000$ sites.
    \item Nodes: 32 distributed cluster nodes.
    \item Defect sweep: $\rho_d \in [0.0, 2.5]$.
    \item Cascade intensity: fixed at $150.0$.
\end{itemize}

\section{Simulation Procedure}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Initialize VQS and distributed tensor fabric.
    \item Construct shard Hamiltonians per node.
    \item Compute singular values and condition numbers.
    \item Log stability status (NOMINAL / WARNING).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Results}
Key observations:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Stable evolution for low $\rho_d$ with moderate $\kappa$.
    \item Threshold behavior near $\rho_d^* \approx 0.74$ indicating phase transition.
    \item Autonomous solver interception when $\kappa > 10^{10}$.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}
The simulations confirm that Apex-TI v4.0 maintains numerical stability and
topological integrity across a wide range of defect densities and cascade
intensities, validating the robustness of the Virtual Quantum Substrate.

